<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav signed_in?={@signed_in?} current={:compare} />

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-400">Compare</p>
        <.marketing_heading tag="h1" scale={:hero} class="mt-2">
          Why not just give the LLM SSH?
        </.marketing_heading>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-8 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
          SSH gives an agent the permissions of its host account. Emisar gives it named
          operations, such as reading service logs or restarting a unit, so it can keep
          investigating while you control which changes run.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-16 max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-sky-300">The same task</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">
          Both approaches run real commands
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
          Both windows run the same rollback. Over SSH, the agent asks to run <code>ssh nomad-prod-1 'nomad job history api'</code>, and one "don't ask again for
          ssh commands" answer lets it run any ssh command after that. With emisar, each call is
          a declared action that emisar checks and records, and the risky
          <code>nomad job revert</code>
          waits until you approve that exact run.
        </p>
      </div>

      <%!-- The difference, shown: the same rollback in the home page's terminal,
           twice. Over SSH one "don't ask again" answer covers every later ssh
           command, so a secret ends up in the chat; through emisar each call is a
           declared action and the revert waits for its own approval. --%>
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-5">
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-rose-300">Raw SSH</p>
          <.emisar_demo script={:raw_ssh} class="mt-3" />
        </div>
        <div class="min-w-0">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-400">With emisar</p>
          <.emisar_demo script={:ssh_emisar} class="mt-3" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-y border-zinc-900 bg-black/20 py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-zinc-400">Side by side</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">
        Where the paths differ
      </h2>
      <%!-- One data list, two layouts: a table on md+, stacked cards on mobile, so
        the 3-way comparison reads without a horizontal scroll on phones. --%>
      <% rows = [
        {"What can the agent run?", "Anything the SSH account can run.",
         "Only the actions in packs you installed. Anything else is rejected."},
        {"How are arguments checked?", "They aren't. The agent writes the whole command line.",
         "Each argument has a type and limits that the runner checks. Sensitive values are masked in the console and API."},
        {"What code runs?", "Whatever is installed on the host at that moment.",
         "The exact pack you trusted. Changed files are blocked until an admin reviews them."},
        {"How does approval work?",
         "A prompt like \"Do you want to run ssh …?\" One \"don't ask again\" covers every later ssh command.",
         "Your policy decides which actions need approval. An approval covers that one run unless you allow reuse, and every decision is recorded."},
        {"What is recorded?", "Login logs. Recording the commands takes a separate tool.",
         "Each run's request, approval, and result in a searchable audit log, plus a host journal you can verify."},
        {"Do secrets reach the model?", "Yes, if the command prints them.",
         "The runner masks secrets that match its rules before output leaves the host."},
        {"How far can it reach?", "Every host that trusts the key, with that account's rights.",
         "Only the runners its member can use. Removing the member cuts off every key that acts as them."},
        {"What if a key leaks?", "Rotate it and remove it from every host that trusts it.",
         "Revoke that one key or OAuth access in the console."}
      ] %>

      <div class="mt-5 hidden overflow-hidden rounded-lg ring-1 ring-white/10 md:block">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-black/40 text-xs text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="w-[22%] px-4 py-3 text-left font-medium">Concern</th>
              <th class="w-[39%] px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-rose-200">Raw SSH</th>
              <th class="w-[39%] px-4 py-3 text-left font-semibold text-brand-300">
                emisar action pack
              </th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/70">
            <tr :for={{concern, raw, emisar} <- rows}>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top font-medium text-zinc-100">{concern}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top leading-6 text-zinc-400">{raw}</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-4 align-top leading-6 text-zinc-300">{emisar}</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-lg bg-zinc-950/70 ring-1 ring-white/10 md:hidden">
        <div
          :for={{concern, raw, emisar} <- rows}
          class="border-b border-zinc-800 p-4 last:border-b-0"
        >
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">{concern}</p>
          <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-sm leading-6">
            <div>
              <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-rose-300">Raw SSH</p>
              <p class="mt-1 text-zinc-400">{raw}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="border-l-2 border-brand-500/40 pl-3">
              <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-400">emisar</p>
              <p class="mt-1 text-zinc-300">{emisar}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-400">For teams</p>
        <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">
          Without one place, AI access over SSH becomes a risky mess
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
          On a team, each engineer who wants an agent on production sets it up their own way,
          usually with an SSH key in the agent's config and a "don't ask again" answer in
          their own chat client.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-5xl divide-y divide-zinc-900 border-y border-zinc-900">
        <div class="py-5 sm:grid sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:gap-8">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-rose-200">Without one place</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty sm:mt-0">
            Each agent can do whatever its key allows on every host that trusts that key.
            Unless you add separate tools for SSH certificates and session recording,
            nobody has a list of which agents hold which keys. Each person sets their own rules, and the
            only shared record is login logs. When a key leaks or someone leaves, you have to
            hunt for it in every authorized_keys file.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="py-5 sm:grid sm:grid-cols-[14rem_1fr] sm:gap-8">
          <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">With emisar</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty sm:mt-0">
            Every agent on the team uses the same reviewed actions under your account's
            policy, and every run is recorded with who asked and where it ran. An agent can
            reach only what its member can, and removing a member cuts off every agent that
            acts as them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <.cta_link
        navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"}
        class="mt-6 inline-block min-h-10 py-2.5 text-sm"
      >Read about teams and access</.cta_link>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[0.72fr_1.28fr]">
        <div class="pb-10 lg:pb-0 lg:pr-12">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-rose-300">For supervised work</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-100 text-balance">
            When raw SSH is the right answer
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-md text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
            A supervised task with a narrowly scoped account can be simpler over SSH,
            especially when your team already operates command restrictions and
            session recording. Throwaway environments can also make a broad shell
            an acceptable tradeoff. Review the account's actual authority first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-t border-zinc-800 pt-10 lg:border-l lg:border-t-0 lg:pl-12 lg:pt-0">
          <p class="text-xs font-semibold text-brand-400">With emisar</p>
          <h2 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold text-zinc-50 text-balance">When it isn't</h2>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400 text-pretty">
            Use Emisar to let your agent keep working with commands you have reviewed.
            The runner checks the inputs on the host, and each run has a record you can
            inspect. Your policy decides what runs automatically, what needs approval,
            and what is denied. These controls work for a solo operator or a team.
          </p>

          <div class="mt-8 max-w-2xl rounded-xl border border-brand-900/40 bg-brand-950/20 p-5 text-sm text-zinc-300">
            Try it: install the runner from the <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >quickstart</.link>, run the included uptime action and inspect its result.
            Then choose one published pack for a task you actually need. The
            <.link navigate={~p"/pricing"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
              free tier
            </.link>
            covers 3 runners.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <nav
        aria-label="Continue comparing emisar"
        class="mt-12 flex flex-col flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-8 gap-y-1 border-t border-zinc-900 pt-6 text-center text-sm sm:flex-row"
      >
        <.cta_link navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"} class="min-h-10 py-2.5">How the trust boundary works</.cta_link>
        <.cta_link tone={:muted} navigate={~p"/use-cases/csi-data-loss"} class="min-h-10 py-2.5">See it on a real incident</.cta_link>
        <.cta_link
          tone={:muted}
          navigate={~p"/guides/give-ai-agents-safe-production-access"}
          class="min-h-10 py-2.5"
        >Guide: safe production access for AI agents</.cta_link>
        <.cta_link
          tone={:muted}
          navigate={~p"/guides/prompt-injection-for-ops-teams"}
          class="min-h-10 py-2.5"
        >Guide: prompt injection for ops teams</.cta_link>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <.marketing_cta
    headline="Stop handing AI agents raw SSH."
    subcopy="Give your agents the commands they need. Set rules for each action and keep a record of what ran."
    secondary_label="Talk to sales"
    secondary_path={EmisarWeb.MailTo.sales()}
  />

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
